Seborrhoeic Keratosis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Stuck-on appearance, Warty/verrucous surface, Well-demarcated pigmented plaque

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-038
Reason for Visit: Acute bleeding and pain from a darkly pigmented skin lesion that caught on clothing, causing severe patient anxiety regarding malignant melanoma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly patient experiencing sudden, continuous bleeding and localized pain from a dark, warty skin lesion on the trunk after it was snagged on clothing. The patient was highly distressed, fearing the lesion was a ruptured skin cancer. Urgent assessment was required to achieve hemostasis, formally assess the lesion to rule out malignancy, and provide immediate clinical reassurance before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "This dark, ugly mole on my back got caught on my shirt and partially ripped off. It's bleeding everywhere, and I'm terrified it's a melanoma that has just burst."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the presence of multiple pigmented lesions on the trunk for several years, noting their multiplicity has been increasing rapidly with advancing age. Specifically, there is a prominent, sharply demarcated exophytic plaque on the right mid-back. The patient notes it has a warty or verrucous surface texture and variable dark black pigmentation. It has been historically asymptomatic but occasionally irritated by clothing friction. Two hours ago, the patient vigorously dried themselves with a towel, accidentally catching the lesion. This resulted in a partial avulsion of the plaque, causing acute localized pain, mild bleeding, and severe anxiety.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety regarding skin cancer.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly anxious, holding a blood-stained tissue to the back.
Physical Exam: The right mid-back displays a 1.5 cm well-demarcated, dark brown to black exophytic plaque. The lesion exhibits the highly characteristic 'stuck-on' appearance, as if pasted onto the skin surface. The superior half of the lesion has been traumatically avulsed, leaving a raw, superficially oozing, erythematous base. The remaining intact inferior portion demonstrates a classic warty/verrucous surface texture. Dermoscopy of the intact portion reveals multiple prominent keratin plugs (pseudohorn cysts) and a complete absence of atypical pigment networks, blue-white veils, or arborizing vessels. There is no deep induration, and no palpable regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is on low-dose aspirin, contributing to the bleeding.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Seborrhoeic Keratosis (Traumatized/Partially Avulsed) - Status: Acute mechanical trauma
Differentials:

Malignant Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic 'stuck-on' waxy appearance, the presence of diagnostic horn cysts on dermoscopy, and the clear history of mechanical avulsion causing the bleeding, rather than spontaneous ulceration of a deeply invasive tumor.

Pigmented Basal Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The verrucous, crumbly surface texture and lack of a pearly, rolled border or translucent quality.

Viral Wart (Verruca Vulgaris) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The truncal location and older age demographic; the lesion lacks the thrombosed capillaries (black dots) typically seen in common warts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (benign condition, high anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an actively bleeding, darkly pigmented truncal lesion. Triage correctly prioritized the active bleeding and severe patient panic regarding melanoma as requiring urgent evaluation. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was vital to achieve mechanical hemostasis. More importantly, performing a thorough clinical and dermoscopic examination allowed for the definitive diagnosis of a benign Seborrhoeic Keratosis. By explicitly ruling out malignant melanoma based on the presence of pseudohorn cysts and the classic 'stuck-on' morphology, the intervention instantly resolved the patient's acute psychological crisis and prevented a highly anxious, unnecessary presentation to an emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved complete hemostasis utilizing firm direct pressure and the application of silver nitrate to the oozing base. Applied a simple, non-adherent protective dressing. Provided extensive clinical reassurance regarding the entirely benign nature of the lesion.
Positive Behavioral Change: By leaving this protective dressing completely intact and dry for the next 3 days, you will allow the torn base of the spot to heal properly, preventing it from catching on your clothes and bleeding again. By understanding that these "age spots" are entirely harmless and distinctly different from skin cancer, you can safely ignore them in the future unless they become repeatedly caught and painful.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks for a general skin check and discussion regarding elective cryotherapy removal of any other irritating lesions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the dressing becomes completely saturated with blood and continues dripping after 15 minutes of firm, uninterrupted direct pressure, or if the area becomes deeply red, hot, and swollen, present to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While clinically this is a classic benign age spot, any pigmented lesion that continues to change, bleed spontaneously, or heal poorly should be formally biopsied by your GP.
Clinical Course: The traumatized base will form a scab and heal over the next 7-10 days. The remaining portion of the lesion will likely remain indefinitely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Apply continuous, firm pressure if the bleeding restarts. Do not peek to see if it has stopped before 15 minutes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This spot is a very common, completely harmless overgrowth of the top layer of skin, often called an "age spot" or a "barnacle." It is not attached deeply, which is why it easily tore and bled when you caught it with the towel. The silver nitrate stopped the bleeding, and it will now heal like a normal graze.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to pull off the remaining part of the spot. Pat your back gently with a towel after showering rather than rubbing vigorously.

SMS INFORMATION

